hey all this is Justin hey Walt reading  dan Starkey's review of Hyrule warriors  definitive edition for Nintendo switch  Hyrule warriors is a beautiful chaotic  mess of a game it's got all the glossy  rupees imaginative monsters and  fashionable characters you'd expect from  the Zelda series and plenty of wooden  topped off with some nods to the  medieval hack-and-slash dynasty warrior  series in place of puzzles and elaborate  levels or side quests you're here to do  one thing mess up some monsters this  shouldn't be new to most folks as the  original run of Hyrule warriors launched  back in 2014 but the ports a Nintendo  switch brings all the extra characters  and items from the DLC plus some added  costumes and all the content from the  3ds version that's a ton of content to  bring to the table but the games central  theme is the same as ever that makes  this switch version a tough sell for all  but the most dedicated fans of the  original or those who never set foot  into the wacky world of this strange  mashup given the Wii U's relatively  meager sales though this is a great  second chance for the strongest Zelda  spin-off ever  for the unfamiliar dynasty warriors is a  tactical action game that tasks you with  managing an army and controlling  specific keeps or tracks of land all the  while you're able to insert yourself  directly into the fray as an uber  powered demigod that allows you to shift  the tide of battle essentially acting as  the Queen in chess powerful though you  may be you've also got to keep constant  track of the field and where you're  needed most that tension between the  battle right in front of you and the  tactical considerations of the field  represent the core tension of the series  Hyrule warriors doesn't compromise on  that at all and even mixes in plenty of  mid game quests and objectives to keep  you juggling your goals and constantly  weighing your best options it's a lot to  have going on at once but it genuinely  works choices are always build as risks  should you go home and shore up the  defenses of your base or press on for a  valuable collectable understanding where  you're needed most and how the various  elements of a map all play together is  important but it's not so taxing that  you can't fudge your way through a good  chunk of it and that's part of the  appeal as a link or da Rooney ax or  Zelda or Impa you've got the entire  Hyrule cast at your back  Zelda isn't some Rando she's a monster  busting fiend even when you've got more  important decisions to make  watching Zelda summon Spears and swords  from brawl light and dispatching wave  after wave of mob lands is the kind of  cathartic release that many have been  waiting decades for this is fanservice  and its most pure and most satisfying  seeing the characters you've grown up  with or idolized in new contexts that  allow them to unleash their full might  is a bit like taking your favorite  characters into Smash Brothers or marvel  vs. capcom there's an essence of  childlike fascination that comes along  with it and hyrule warriors wields that  well  [Music]  fans of Zelda lore and the like need not  turn their noses up at this adventure  either provided you can buy into the  initial premise and get some mileage  there The Adventure is truly a  fascinating one you'll cross dimensions  and time lines bouncing between locales  from many of the more recent Zelda  entries including Wind Waker breadth of  the wild and Skyward Sword all of this  fits tonally 2 with more than a dozen  characters from all over the timeline in  Zelda's own history of world swapping  time warping weirdness muddling the  timelines between worlds a bit to get  everyone into the same games feels  natural if anything as we stated in our  original review the one major issue this  brings up is the longing for flash sheer  attacks and better combos in the  mainline Zelda series and when a  spin-off makes you want more from the  original that's certainly a special sort  of accomplishment new to the switch  specifically though a split-screen  multiplayer the original allowed one  player to use the gamepad and another to  play on the TV this mode honestly while  nice isn't much of an improvement this  switch can still chug a bit when the  action gets heavy and trying to pack two  players and the mayhem they caused into  a single screen feels a little tight  still it's always nice to have the  option those returning to the fray will  likely be disappointed as there just  isn't enough to satisfy the full retail  price tag for newcomers though Hyrule  warriors is a delightful bizarre outing  that opens up the Zelda series taking us  places we've been before  just this time with thousands of  monsters and awesome screen clearing  magical blasts  [Music]
 

